Opt-in EFI framebuffer KMS display control
========================================

The aarch64 EFIFB build option supplies efifb_drm.ko and the shared helpers
needed for firmware scanout. The pinned upstream archive and original ports
patches remain intact. The additional patch retains upstream licenses and
applies only with EFIFB enabled; amd64 keeps its preceding source selection.

This driver exposes the existing fixed EFI mode through a primary DRM node
and shmem dumb buffers. It performs CPU copies, reports no accelerated render
node, does not change HDMI timings, and is not a native VC4/V3D driver. The
build option defaults on for aarch64; runtime activation defaults off.

Activation requires hw.efifb_drm.enable=1 before loading the modules during
rc startup, after kernel subsystem initialization. Do not preload the modules
from loader.conf. The private hwtrace image control loads lindebugfs,
linuxkpi_hdmi, linuxkpi_video, dmabuf, drm and efifb_drm in that order. Do not
enable firmware scanout alongside another active driver for that display.
Unloading a published display is refused because devfs teardown is unqualified.

Original native controls passed 16,404 layout/bounds checks and actual KMS
modeset, damage, page-flip event and console restoration on ARM64 QEMU and
the Pi 4. The Pi operator confirmed the color pattern. Actual software
Wayland terminal pixels and compositor IPC were also observed. These were
module/image controls, not tests of this newly packaged artifact. Native
ports stage/package/independent installation acceptance must be recorded
separately before package qualification.

Run the port's original test target to compile and execute the layout checks.
tests/test_efi_kms_device.c supplies explicit attended device controls; it
must not run automatically during package build/install. The preserved
VirtIO custom framebuffer path has separate unchecked GEM-size validation
work outstanding; the EFI negative tests do not qualify that path.

Physical Pi 5, native GPU acceleration, WiFi, full memory, the complete
Daemonarchy session and update/rollback remain independent release gates.
